course: dictionary
lesson: 3125
title: Electrons: tiny charged particles
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An electron is a tiny particle, much smaller than an atom. It has a negative electric charge and moves around the middle of an atom, called the nucleus. «An atom is made of smaller parts: protons and neutrons in a tiny middle called the nucleus, and electrons around it.» So the nucleus is the centre, and the electrons are the parts around it.
explain: When many electrons flow through a wire, that flow is an electric current. «Metals let electricity flow because they have loose electrons that can drift along; plastic, rubber and wood block it.» Metals let electrons flow easily, so they are called conductors. Plastic and rubber do not, so they are called insulators.
explain: Electrons do not only work in circuits. Rubbing a balloon on your hair moves electrons from one thing to another, and that is static electricity. In solar panels, «light hits thin slices of silicon and knocks tiny electrons loose», so they flow away as electricity.
fact: An electron is a tiny particle with a negative electric charge.
fact: Electrons are much smaller than an atom and move around its nucleus, the centre.
fact: When many electrons flow through a wire, that flow is an electric current.
fact: Metals are conductors because electrons can flow through them easily; plastic and rubber are insulators.
fact: «Electron microscopes use beams of electrons instead of light» to show things thousands of times smaller than a light microscope can, such as pollen grains.
exercise: «An atom is made of smaller parts: protons and neutrons in a tiny middle called the nucleus, and electrons around it.» Where are the electrons in an atom? | Inside the wire | Inside the nucleus with the protons | Outside the atom completely | *Around the nucleus
exercise: What is an electric current? | A rubber glove | Light shining from a lamp | A metal spring that bounces | *Many electrons flowing through a wire
exercise: «Metals let electricity flow because they have loose electrons that can drift along.» Which of these is a conductor? | Wooden ruler | Rubber glove | Plastic cup | *Metal spoon
test: «Electron microscopes use beams of electrons instead of light.» What do electron microscopes use instead of light? | Magnets | *Beams of electrons | Mirrors | Sound waves
test: Rubbing a balloon on your hair moves electrons from one thing to another. What is this called? | Insulation | Solar power | Friction flow | *Static electricity
exam: Which statement about an electron is true? | *It has a negative electric charge and is much smaller than an atom | It is a type of metal | It has no electric charge at all | It is bigger than an atom
